 in green energy  technology in the uk shell plans to  invest between 20 and 25 billion pounds  in the uk energy system over the next  decade and more than 75 percent of that  in low carbon energy with the rest going  into uk gas and oil supplies including  in the north sea and bp has said it will  invest 18 billion pounds in britain in  the great majority in offshore wind  solar hydrogen carbon capture and  storage and of course electric vehicle  charging well sky's business  correspondent helen ann smith was  speaking to the chancellor this morning  in stoke-on-trent what we heard from the  chancellor today on whimple taxes was  really some of the softer language we've  heard from him on the record on the  subject you might be wondering why i'm  surrounded by plates and what this has  got to do with windfall taxes i'm at  royal stafford it's a pottery and  ceramics factory in stoke on trent it  crucially is a very high energy  intensive business uses a huge amount of  energy this company alone is  renegotiating its energy contract at the  moment and their energy bills could  potentially go up as much as 400 or even  600 so  how do we answer some of these questions  of huge energy prices for businesses and  for individuals as well well one of the  options on the table that labor in  particular have been proposing is the  windfall tax to uh tax the very specific  excessive profits that have been made  recently for from bp and shell  and other such companies have made a lot  of money out of these high energy prices  the chancellor and the prime minister  certainly up until now have been  resisting it and the reason they say is  they don't want to deter  investment into the uk particularly  green investment  if they were to put these taxes on uh  however the language  from uh some of the oil and gas  companies has softened a little on that  bernard looney uh last week indicated  that he would uh certainly retain some  of the commitments he's already made  but perhaps was a little softer in his  language on whether he would withdraw  other investment commitments as well so  perhaps in that context uh the  chancellor feels that the it will be  easier for him uh to act if you like the  other reason perhaps is that pressure on  him really really growing the cost of  living crisis getting  excessively worse forecasts from the  bank of england last week that the  economy will contract that there could  be a recession that inflation is going  to exceed 10  uh really a sense that the prime  minister wants to do more as well it may  get to a stage where the chancellor  cannot resist big action like this  anymore and a windfall tax certainly an  option on the table and one he's  speaking softer about now  now for some of the day's other climate  news now because new research has found  extreme weather could help replenish  shorelines eroded by rising sea levels  caused by climate change now academics  from australia the u.s the uk also found  huge large waves can drag sand from  beaches and it dumps it along the  surrounding coastline and global warming  is also expected to increase  wave heights during extreme weather  events along with almost three fifths of  the world's coastline by the end  century but new findings could  potentially change long-term predictions  about the future of our coastlines  a record-breaking number of companies  across 70 countries have set emission  reduction targets according to a climate  think tank the report  from the science-based targets  initiative says more than 2 000 firms  have joined the initiative but the  report says there's still work to be  done highlighting that companies in asia  africa and latin america are lagging  behind europe the u.s and japan  octopus energy group has announced a new  partnership with aims to deliver wind  and solar energy from morocco to the uk  by 2027 x-links plans to lay the world's  largest sub-sea cables to connect a huge  renewable energy farm in the moroccan  desert with devon now the project is  expected to improve uk energy security  and deliver reliable clean power for an  average of 20 hours a day that's enough  energy to power about 7 million heat  pumps all year round  now wildfires continue to wreak havoc in  the united states as the country's  largest in new mexico forced evacuations  from a mountain resort and flames also  whipped through a coastal area in  southern california destroying several  homes now the area was extremely dry and  flammable but hadn't burnt for years but  the local police chief put the blame  squarely on climate change  the winds we experienced today are not  unusual these were not santa ana winds  these are uh our coastal winds that we  experience generally every day  the big difference is and we're seeing  it again  you know with with the climate change  the fuels  beds in this county throughout southern  california throughout the west are so  dry  that you know fire like this is going to  be more commonplace  nbc's steve patterson reports now from  laguna niguel in southern california  as the sun is coming up now here in  orange county this appears to be  what amounts to a hot spot operation  firefighters going from home to home in  this multi-million dollar neighborhood  to make sure that all the smoldering and  the hot spots are contained the fire  though was absolutely devastating you  can see the home behind me this is one  of several in this neighborhood a  neighborhood of multi-million dollar  ruins i'm seeing maybe every fifth home  on this block in my eyesight has been  completely reduced to rubble it is  extreme the nature and the intensity and  the speed of this fire firefighters say  as of about five o'clock last night this  fire was maybe three acres maybe it was  five acres then firefighters watched it  jump five acres by five acres until it  was about 40 acres and then leaping to  hundreds of acres the fire racing up  bone dry heavy vegetation on us on the  side of a canyon here the fire cresting  over that canyon leaping with embers  onto rooftops in this neighborhood and  then those embers leap from roof to roof  to roof as wind gust of about 30 miles  per hour driven by these coastal ocean  winds leap from house to house really  destroying at least 20 homes uh in this  neighborhood alone firefighters working  on containment now but it is clear as it  is only may extremely early for this  time of year here in california that  climate change is driving a lot of this  especially how dry the vegetation is  exacerbating that mega drought that this  state has been in for so long the state  in a water crisis only further making  fire conditions worse and when you have  a situation like that with the  superheated temperatures that will come  later in the summer california residents  are already bracing for what could come  and what has already happened we've seen  here very unusual very bizarre for a  fire this small to move this fast this  early in the season firefighters working  on cleanup working to get people back to  see their homes but for now it's a it's  an operation to try to keep people away  from this area as they work on the rest  of the hot spots  now mangroves are a powerful natural  solution to climate change the trees  which grow along coastal wetlands are  not only a huge carbon store  counteracting emissions they also  protect vulnerable communities from  storm surges but as we'll hear some  residents of puerto rico are battling to  save some mangroves which are at risk  from urban development which is against  the law take a look  a  [Music]  [Music]  a  [Music]  and a bit of slightly unexpected news  despite global warming and some ice ice  shelves in eastern antarctic have grown  over the last 20 years now this is  research published in the journal nature  geoscience it's suggesting that the  growth was produced by changes in wind  direction which pushed sea ice up  against the shelves now the ice shelves  were found to have grown between 2003  and 2019 but since then an increasing  number of icebergs have broken away from  the eastern antarctic peninsula  well do stay with us coming up  i'll be joined by dr shihu who works  with oxford's environmental change  institute and richard black an honorary  fellow with the grantham institution  will be asking how oil and gas companies  can be persuaded  to reduce emissions see you soon  [Music]  so jim jim travels quite frequently uh  on sort of geology and archaeology  interest trips with other hobbyists  um and  travel to iraq with an organized tour  group around a variety of historical  sites and mosques and things as well as  sort of desert desert ruins and things  in iraq um and on visiting a site called  eredu  found some pieces of broken stone and  shards of broken pottery on the ground  kind of asked whether it would be all  right if he takes a few home to stick on  the bookshelf at home as souvenirs of  the trip  um he was told that that would be fine  because they have no historical or  economic value that was confirmed by  the sort of experienced tour group that  he was with and also by a iraqi ministry  tourism official and uh a former police  officer who was with them as security  and then upon arriving at the airport  jim was arrested and charged with  attempting to smuggle historical  artifacts out of the country and that  was eight weeks ago he's still in  detention at the airport so we've  received no kind of ministerial  engagement of any kind um we've we've  never spoken directly with any anyone  with sort of political decision making  capability but the the fcdo are very  keen to to sort of point out and we  acknowledge they are providing consular  support it's worth calling out what that  is that's that is uh weekly or you know  every other week visit to jim to make  sure that he's been fed and watered that  is providing us with the phone number of  an english-speaking lawyer in iraq  um and  that's about it so so you know i mean  under the assumption that the foreign  office looks after british citizens who  run afoul of  injustice abroad um you know we feel  like this could certainly be doing more  and just you know we are just urging the  minister to just meet with us to discuss  ways in which you know they they are  helping or or all ways in which they can  help  we've not spoken verbally since since  before he was arrested so let's say just  just coming up on eight weeks now um but  he gets online uh our time about one am  for for between five and ten minutes  there's a flurry of messages and he sort  of picks up what we've been sending him  throughout the day and replies a few  times and then disappears again but  as i said the consular staff on the  ground have been to visit him and they  assure us that he is being part of food  and water and sort of you know his basic  needs are being looked after but you  know it's eight weeks in a temporary  holding cell in an airport in baghdad  can't be uh can't be comfortable of  course  [Music]  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news so it's time  now to discuss some of those climate  issues of the day with dr shihu and  environmental consultant uh richard  black thank you both for joining us this  evening so i guess the first big topic  then despite promises and pledges made  at the un climate conference in glasgow  oil and gas companies are well they're  ruling the roost vast profits as we've  heard and according to reporting in the  guardian this week huge ongoing  investments in projects that could see  carbon emissions  spiral out of control well richard to  you first are you hopeful then that cop  27 when it comes around will help drive  that conversation to deter oil and gas  companies from developing  i think it may play a role but the  reality is there are a number of  countries that are not you know  themselves convinced of the need to cut  back on oil and gas production right now  and unsurprisingly those come those  countries include the ones that produce  the most oil and gas and  i don't think that any international  agreements really are going to make them  do that i think the real uh secret the  real way that the the output of  oil and gas companies is going to be  undermined the main way anyway is  basically by them being undercut on  price  and if you look at what's happening in  the energy industry  there is virtually no country on earth  now where it's cheaper to build and  operate a new gas fire power station  than it is to build and operate a new  solar farm or a new wind farm  and in many cases it's actually cheaper  to build a new solar farm and operate it  than it is to carry on with an existing  power station that you have  meanwhile in the electric car  field which is the main you know way to  undercut oil demand sales are going  bananas we're talking about you know  each year the number of sales going up  by maybe 40 percent  compounded so they just keep going up  and in the end this is just i think  going to eat the market of oil and gas  companies and cc i guess there's a big  drive um like richard said to invest in  green energy isn't there  and that's been pushed on i suppose by  the war in ukraine um is there still  room though for those big countries the  middle east the u.s australia canada to  understand where green energy should be  yes i think so um although i think what  really matters is the content of the  talks in these sort of um climate talks  and diplomacies right so the problem is  there is a fundamental mismatch between  the promises that are being made by the  countries um and the market incentives  so if we're going back to you know oil  and gas companies at any company they  are driven by making money so what we  should be thinking about is whether  these governments who are engaging in  climate talks and diplomacy have the  economic tools to change the behavior of  these companies because ultimately  they're here to 