 you you get  to net zero  okay that leads us really nicely really  on to our next big topic which is  targets um because we know uh that a  record number of big polluting companies  are committing to cutting uh co2  emissions and this is all from a un  backed report and then separately to  that they've cast out on whether oil  companies can kind of deliver on those  targets too so richard i'll start with  you how accountable are these top  companies when it comes to you know big  grand gestures and big targets that they  do put out there  yes most of the companies that are  looked at here are the big publicly are  publicly traded companies and there are  all kinds of sectors you know whether it  be retail um manufacturing all kinds of  things ultimately they're accountable to  their to their to their shareholders and  of course we've seen in recent years a  number of times shelled as you know  passing resolutions that basically make  the directors uh pay more attention to  this stuff a lot of the time they'll be  beholden to their customers  because you know particularly in europe  uh there is a tradition of companies  decide sorry customers deciding who they  will buy from based on the behavior of  that particular company and then the  other thing that's coming up on the  rails is really regulation and in many  countries uh and jurisdictions um  companies are increasingly have to  having to disclose what their climate  risk is for example so all these things  are playing a role but it's not there's  no sort of monolithic solution here it's  going to be incremental it's going to be  messy and not all companies and all  places are going to have to toe the line  at the same time  cece we do know that more than half of  these companies setting targets are in  the g7 rich nations don't we but  india china brazil they're also in the  mix it's quite encouraging that isn't it  yes i think it's it's really um  essential that companies are actually um  now making pledges um because carbon  production is um you know at the company  level ultimately um although i i agree  with richard  everything that he said i think i'm  gonna throw in a bit of a  pessimistic thing in terms of a  challenge here i think um companies um  don't necessarily know how to quantify  emissions and then i think that's one of  the key problems right now right even if  you pledge um if you can't quantify and  you don't know where your missions are  coming from then how are you supposed to  come up with the best practices how  you're supposed to prioritize how are  you supposed to evaluate most  importantly of your progress um oil and  gas companies in particular uh you know  struggle with leaks um so there's a lot  of challenges around these you know  pipelines running thousands of miles and  nobody knows how much gas greenhouse  gases is you know leaked um so yeah do  companies have the capacity to measure  emissions properly that's one of the key  challenges um  but yeah there is progress to be made um  it is about trust it's about  accountability but one thing that is  clear richard mark carney very briefly  is warning governments they can't derail  the climate process um  with the war in ukraine do you agree  yes i do but you have to say that for  many governments that otherwise i think  would be saying strong things about the  climate talks at the moment they're not  because their attention is focused on  ukraine and it's not just the conflicts  as well we've seen the energy  prices around the world affected we're  going to see food prices around the  world affected so these are all visceral  things in europe we're actually looking  i think at accelerating decarbonization  because people are now realizing  politicians are now realizing that in  the words of the german finance minister  christian lindner renewable energy is  freedom energy and on that note we'll  leave it thank you cece and richard a  pleasure talking to you today well do  stay with us coming 